; jmpr_fall2.simi — M2.13: CASCADING fall-through folds. Three JMPRs in a
; row, each folding to its OWN next pc (r1=5 -> pc 5, r2=6 -> pc 6,
; r3=7 -> pc 7). All three index constants are loaded BEFORE the chain
; (pcs 1-3), which is what makes the cascade bite: under M2.12's merge,
; the first fold's rule-1 mark on pc 5 reset the constant map at the
; SECOND JMPR's index (r2, loaded at pc 2), un-folding it — and a
; JMPR that fails to fold is DYNAMIC, which kills g_alloc for the whole
; function (the other two JMPRs fall back to the runtime table + bounds
; check). M2.13's fix: the fold fixpoint's rule-1 merge skips fall-
; through targets (target == pc+1 — their branch is the dead one M2.12
; drops), so the constant map flows through all three joins, every JMPR
; folds, and the coalescing pre-pass fuses all three block boundaries:
; no branch, no boundary flush, x9/x10/x11 resident across the whole
; chain — the register frame is loaded once, not four times.
; A regression to the mark-all merge is caught two ways: the size gate
; (three dynamic table paths vs. three dropped branches) and correctness
; (the dynamic path bounds-check must still produce 18 — the parity
; engines prove the runtime value).
; Expected result: 18
.entry main
main:
    ENTER #8
    LOADI r1, #5, i64        ; index for JMPR@4 -> folds to pc 5 = pc+1
    LOADI r2, #6, i64        ; index for JMPR@5 -> folds to pc 6 = pc+1
    LOADI r3, #7, i64        ; index for JMPR@6 -> folds to pc 7 = pc+1
    JMPR r1                  ; fall-through fold #1 (chain head)
    JMPR r2                  ; fall-through fold #2 (chain middle)
    JMPR r3                  ; fall-through fold #3 (chain tail)
    ADD r0, r1, r2, i64      ; r0 = 5 + 6 = 11
    ADD r0, r0, r3, i64      ; r0 = 11 + 7 = 18
    RET
